  "alive-signals": {
    how: [
      "There are four signals a page can use today, and each one notices a different set of endings.",
      "The goodbye. A page that is closing, navigating or being removed from its parent fires <code>pagehide</code>, and can post on a port from there. It is the quickest signal, a few milliseconds. It is never sent by a worker, by a process that crashes, or by a page that just calls <code>close()</code>.",
      "Pings and beats. They notice everything, including a stuck thread and a crash, and they are the only signal that works across origins for anything other than a page. Their cost is time: the far side is only called gone after several missed intervals. A stuck far side looks the same as a dead one until it recovers.",
      "A Web Lock. The far side requests a lock with a name only it uses and never releases it. The page queues for the same name. The browser grants the page's request the moment the holder is destroyed: frame removed, page navigated, worker terminated, process crashed. It does not fire for a stuck thread, which still holds its lock, or for <code>close()</code> on a port. Locks belong to one origin, and in a frame on another site, to one storage partition, so this only works between contexts that share both.",
      "The port <code>close</code> event. It would have been the quickest of all. It is no longer in the HTML standard and no browser turns it on. The column stays so that a browser that does have it shows its numbers.",
      "Only on this page, the far side for the busy row is a worker. A frame on the same site as the page usually shares its thread, so a busy frame stops the page too, and the page cannot time anything while it waits.",
    ],
    uses: ["Choosing the signal for each far side: a goodbye and a lock for pages and workers on your own origin, pings or beats for everything else."],
    tryIt: ["Run every row and compare the goodbye and lock columns with the pings and beats columns.", "In Chrome, start it with <code>--enable-blink-features=MessagePortCloseEvent</code> and run the table again. The last column fills in.", "Run it on a phone too, where the timings are different."],
    traps: ["A Web Lock used as a liveness signal must have a name nobody else will use. Make it from <code>crypto.randomUUID()</code>.", "A held Web Lock can stop a page entering the back/forward cache in some browsers, and the page's code cannot release it while frozen. Release it in <code>pagehide</code> if you hold one in a page.", "A lock on another origin has the same name and is a different lock. The page gets it at once and would conclude, wrongly, that the far side is gone."],
    measured: "Chromium 153, frames on this origin: removing the frame was seen by the goodbye in 3 to 4 ms and by the lock in 4 to 5 ms. Navigating it away, 16 to 31 ms for both. <code>close()</code> was seen only by pings (1.8 s) and beats (1.5 s). A terminated worker's lock was free in 1 ms. A worker busy for 4 seconds was marked silent by pings and beats and alive again afterwards, and kept its lock. With the <code>MessagePortCloseEvent</code> flag on, the close event fired 0 to 1 ms after <code>close()</code>, 2 to 4 ms after removal, 20 to 24 ms after navigation and 1 ms after a worker was terminated, and not for a busy worker. Between two origins, a page was granted the far side's lock name immediately while the far side still held it.",
    related: ["health-pair", "health-beats", "alive-restart", "exchange"],
  },
